  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Immunization is the process of making a person resistant to infectious diseases, typically via vaccines that stimulate the immune system to recognize and fight pathogens like bacteria or viruses. It is a critical, highly effective public health tool that prevents 3.5–5 million deaths annually, protecting against diseases like measles, COVID-19, and tetanus. How It Works & Types Active Immunization (Vaccines): Introduces a weakened, inactive, or partial germ (mRNA, protein subunits) to teach the body to produce antibodies without causing the disease. Passive Immunization: Provides immediate protection by giving antibodies directly (e.g., immunoglobulins) rather than relying on the body to produce them. Types of Vaccines: Included are live-attenuated (measles/mumps/rubella), inactivated (polio), subunit/conjugate (HPV, Hib), and mRNA (some COVID-19). Purpose & Benefits Individual Protection: Prepares the immune system for future exposure, reducing the risk of severe illness or death. Herd Immunity: Protects vulnerable populations who cannot be vaccinated (e.g., infants, immunocompromised) by limiting the disease spread in the community. Disease Eradication: Has eliminated or significantly reduced diseases like smallpox and polio. Side Effects & Safety Vaccines are safe and thoroughly tested. Common, mild side effects: Pain, swelling, or redness at the injection site, low-grade fever, headache, and fatigue. These are temporary and signs the body is building protection. Common Vaccinations Children: DTaP (diphtheria, tetanus, pertussis), MMR, Polio, Hib, Varicella, Hepatitis B. Adults: Influenza, COVID-19, Shingles, Tdap. For a personalized schedule or to check for specific risks, it is best to consult with a medical provider.",
